\strong{Groups and models.} \emph{Group methods} compare the samples of one
categorical variable: \code{groups}, a column name of \code{data} or one value per
sample. \emph{Model methods} fit a linear model, \code{formula}, of any number of
variables; \code{groups} names its term of interest (the one their plots and
post-hoc tests use by default), and \code{groups} alone is the same as
\code{formula = ~ groups}. So \code{da_test(counts, m, 'status', meta)} compares
the samples by \code{status} for every method \code{m}, and covariates go in
\code{formula}: \code{da_test(counts, 'da_lm', 'status', meta, ~ status + age)}.
A one-variable \code{formula} works for the group methods too; more than one
variable is an error for them.

The methods' assumptions differ, and so do their results: see each
method's page. In benchmarks on real data (Nearing et al. 2022; Wirbel
et al. 2024; Pelto et al. 2025), the plain per-feature tests
(\code{\link[=da_wilcoxon]{da_wilcoxon()}}, \code{\link[=da_ttest]{da_ttest()}}, \code{\link[=da_lm]{da_lm()}}) controlled false discoveries
well, and the methods often disagree: a feature found by several methods
is a firmer finding than one found by one.

\strong{Flexible name matching}

Case insensitive and partial matching, ignoring anything but letters
(digits count only to tell \code{'ANCOM-BC2'} from \code{'ANCOM-BC'}). E.g.
\code{method = 'Wilcoxon'}, \code{'Mann-Whitney'} or \code{'rank sum'} selects
\code{da_wilcoxon}; \code{'t-test'} or \code{'Welch'} selects \code{da_ttest}; \code{'lm'} or
\code{'linear model'} selects \code{da_lm}; and \code{'ALDEx2'} selects
\code{dirmult_ttest}, an ALDEx2-style method (not ALDEx2 itself, whose
results agree with it in direction and rough size only).

Finished code should always use the full primary option name to avoid
ambiguity with future additions to the list of methods.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

None known.
